Deployed 30-chunk paced runs (batch 10, 8 s delay) for both models also had 0 x 429 (RUN for `-001`, USER-verified for `-2`). No 429 occurred in any streaming run (section 5.4).

**Not captured:** the 429 response body and headers, any `Retry-After` header or `retryDelay` value, and the name of the quota that was exceeded. The earlier unpaced result was not kept at that level of detail (harness gaps, section 1). **No deliberate 429 test was performed**; Joshua declined one.

**What this supports:** in these tests, pacing (smaller batches with a pause) avoided the failures. The unpaced run changed batch size and delay together, each configuration ran once, and the console figures at the failure (RPM 100/100, TPM 15.41K/30K) are consistent with a per-minute request quota but do not identify it. **The exact quota responsible remains unknown**, and so does the exact cause of the 429 responses. Nothing here describes 429 behaviour on the LLM side or on Groq (none was observed).

**Consequence for the adapter (design note, no code changed):** keep ingest paced and retry only on 429 and 5xx with a bounded wait that honours `Retry-After` or `retryDelay` when present, since their presence is unconfirmed.

