### Measurements to record per test

Model id; date; HTTP statuses; per-batch and total latency; time to first token; total stream time; whether text arrived incrementally; vector length and norm; finish reason; reported token usage; any `retry-after` and `x-ratelimit-*` values; the console's before/after usage.

## 6. Decision rules and where the evidence stands

The rules were written before the results. Status below covers `gemini-embedding-001` (RUN) and `gemini-embedding-2` (USER-verified, section 5.6). The embedding decision is recorded in `docs/decisions.md` (`gemini-embedding-2`, 768 dimensions).

| Rule                                                                                                 | Status on 2026-10-31                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1. 768-length, finite vectors in the probe, a local batch and a deployed batch                       | Met for `-001` (probe is USER-reported; local 100 and deployed 30 are RUN)                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
| 2. Right chunk ranked first for all 5 answerable questions; record the unanswerable question's score | Met locally (5/5) for both `-001` and `-2` (`-2`: USER-verified). Unanswerable top-1 similarity recorded: 0.6776 (see observations). Deployed retrieval not run                                                                                                                 |
| 3. Prefer a stable model; if tied, prefer fewer manual steps                                         | **Met (USER).** `-2` is reported as stable and generally available (announced 2026-04-22, section 4). On this synthetic set the two models tie on dimensions, retrieval (5/5) and 429s; `-2` needs no manual normalization and `-001` does. `-001`'s own status is not recorded |
| 4. If vectors are not unit length, the adapter must L2-normalize before storing                      | Not triggered for `-2` (norms 1.0 locally and deployed, USER-verified). Triggered for `-001` (about 0.58-0.59). **Decided: no additional normalization with `-2`**                                                                                                              |
| 5. If 768 is impossible or a 30-page ingest is impractical, recommend an alternative                 | 768 works for both models. Measured on synthetic data: `-2` 100 chunks in 108.392 s (about 18.4 s active), `-001` 109 s. A real 30-page PDF has not been run, so the 60 s target is **not shown either way** (see below). Rule 5 is not triggered by the evidence               |
| 6. `vector(768)` in DATA_MODEL stays unless rule 5 triggers                                          | Unchanged: `vector(768)` stays. No migration is written                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |

Ingest time note (OPEN for real documents): the 109 s (`-001`) and 108.392 s (`-2`) each include 90 s of deliberate waiting; active time was about 18 s for 100 synthetic chunks. The pace used (about 55 texts per minute) was chosen to be safe, not tuned, and how fast it can safely go is unknown. How many chunks a real 30-page PDF produces is unknown. These are measurements on synthetic text, not a claim about a real PDF, and both unknowns decide whether the 60 s target is reachable.

## 7. Unresolved questions

- `gemini-embedding-2`: quota accounting (needs a console before/after reading around a run), retrieval on the deployed route, and real-PDF ingest performance. Its stable status is USER-reported (section 4). The embedding decision itself is recorded; the LLM provider roles are separate and still provisional.
- If the embedding model is ever switched back to `-001`, or a different dimension is used, vectors must be re-checked for length and all chunks re-embedded (the model id is stored per chunk).
- Adapter requirement (not implemented; no code changed): for `gemini-embedding-2` apply the document prefix at ingest and the query prefix at search time, store the exact model id per chunk, and apply no `taskType` and no extra L2 normalization (section 5.6).
- Retrieval quality on real documents and questions. The only evidence is 6 questions over synthetic text. Whether a score cutoff or the first-to-second gap should drive refusals is unresolved.
- The exact Gemini quota accounting (per text or per request, and whether rejected calls count). Needs a console before/after reading around a paced run.
- The real 429 response (status per attempt, whether `Retry-After` or a `retryDelay` is sent, which quota is named) was never captured, and the exact quota responsible is unknown (section 5.7). Accepted as a recorded limitation to close Task 0.5. It stays open unless a 429 is observed passively; no deliberate probe was run.
- 429 behaviour on the LLM side and on Groq (not observed). Deliberate probing needs Joshua's explicit approval.
- Multi-second streaming through Vercel. The four short streams show no evidence of whole-response buffering but do not prove incremental delivery. Task 0.8 is outstanding.
- Not reviewed or not reported: the short-prompt streaming runs, and Gemma 4 and qwen results. Also untested: answer quality beyond one question, LLM-side 429 behaviour, concurrent requests, mid-stream failure and switching to the fallback, and Groq's reasoning-token cost on harder questions.
- Groq output normalization (citation brackets `【n】`, markdown bold, U+202F) is required before citation validation. Not implemented; no code changed.
- Where the 0.9-1.1 s client-minus-server overhead comes from (network path, connection setup, function start-up).
- Gemini as provisional primary LLM and Groq as fallback remain **provisional**, not a final decision. The embedding provider (Gemini Embedding 2) is selected.
- Scope of free-tier use (USER, 2026-10-31): acceptable during development with synthetic or non-sensitive documents only. For a real product, paid-tier privacy and data-handling requirements must be reassessed, including the EEA restriction recorded in section 4.
- Whether either provider needs a card for ongoing use, and whether Groq trains on free-tier inputs.
- Provider limits and terms can change. The limits tables and DOC rows are from 2026-10-30 (console snapshots again on 2026-10-31) and are not re-verified here.
- The deployed 100-chunk case (not tested; the route's 40 s budget cannot fit a paced run), and latency from the Vercel function region (`iad1`, assumed from the header) to each provider.
- Harness gaps listed in section 1 (no code changed).

## 8. Cleanup when the spike is finished

- Delete `app/api/spike/route.ts`; keep `spikes/provider/` until task 10.x ("Move or delete /spikes").
- Rotate or delete `SPIKE_TOKEN` in Vercel and remove `GEMINI_API_KEY`/`GROQ_API_KEY` from the Vercel project only if they are no longer needed (they will be needed from Slice 1).
- Do not commit `spikes/provider/results/`.
